{"body": "The recorded observation reports PROGRAM_NOT_JSON while parsing the commitments blob at character 1124, so the candidate never ran and no execution evidence exists about its behavior. Separating the three layers: (1) the actual program text, (2) the prose account, (3) the execution evidence. The observation is an interpreter/construction failure, which is a translation failure and not proof that the prose account is false. The prose account itself describes a coherent mechanism: a per-booking register keyed by booking id, replaced only when the incoming revision is strictly greater than the stored revision, followed by classifying the latest event (hold with expires_at > now contributes quantity, else zero; release contributes zero), summing by item, and emitting all stock items sorted by name with capacity minus reserved. That mechanism, if implemented faithfully, would satisfy duplicate idempotence, revision order over delivery order, expiry without resurrection of older holds, cancellation by higher-revision release, and visible oversubscription via negative availability. However, the program text does not appear to implement the stated mechanism: the fold accumulator handling is malformed. The inner map's if test compares the stored value at the booking key with the literal string \"__never__\" using lt, the 'then' branch returns the event rather than assigning into an accumulator, and there is no actual state threading from one event to the next (the accumulator name is bound to an empty object and used as if it were being updated, but the value never changes). Even if the JSON were syntactically valid, this would compute the same constant result regardless of events, so it would produce reserved=0 for every item and never accumulate contributions. That is a concrete mismatch between the prose account (which claims a revision-comparing fold) and the program (which lacks the revision comparison and the state update). The smallest distinguishing case is the duplicate_delivery example: three identical revision-1 holds for booking b on widget, capacity 10. The prose account predicts reserved=3, available=7; the program as written would predict reserved=0, available=10, because no booking registry is populated. The cause is a construction failure in the fold: the accumulator is initialized to {} and never mutated, the comparison uses a nonsense sentinel instead of the stored event's revision, and the if returns an event or the old accumulator rather than the new state. Because the observed run is a syntax error, I cannot claim the program is disproven in execution; what I can say is that the evidence does not support the prose account, and inspection of the program text shows the mechanism is not realized. I would not say 'no defect' here: there is a demonstrated construction defect (invalid JSON wrapper and a non-functional fold), separate from the absence of successful execution evidence. Falsification conditions for the prose account: an event for an unlisted item causing failure, same booking+revision with conflicting payloads, non-integer or non-comparable revisions, inclusive expiry semantics, or arithmetic overflow. Successful behavior any fix must preserve: duplicate deliveries do not double-count, strictly higher revision wins regardless of delivery order, expired latest hold does not revive older holds, latest release zeroes the booking, all stock items appear sorted ascending with zero default, and negative availability is emitted rather than clamped.", "commitments": "If the JSON wrapper is repaired and the fold is rewritten as a true per-booking register that stores the latest event by maximum revision and then classifies hold-vs-release with strict expires_at > now, I will emit a sorted list of every stock item with reserved equal to the sum of active-latest-quantity contributions and available equal to capacity minus reserved, allowing negative values. I will treat the current observation as a construction failure rather than a behavioral refutation, and will not claim the prose account has been executed successfully until a runnable program produces matching results on the public cases."}